      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Harding Lake spans 2,470 acres, while Birch Lake covers 841 acres, both offering clear waters that support lake trout and arctic char.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        22,
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/harding_birch_lakes_alaska",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Winter lows can plunge to -50°F, while July highs rarely exceed 72°F, keeping the interior subarctic climate cool and crisp.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7,
        25
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/harding_birch_lakes_alaska",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Semi‑nomadic groups set up temporary summer fish camps and winter villages, living in small bands of 20‑40 people for millennia in the Tanana Valley lowlands.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/harding_birch_lakes_alaska",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The area hosts moose, black bears, and migratory waterfowl like loons, thriving in spruce and birch forests around the lakes.",
